\begin{didyouknow}{Euclid's Endless Primes}
Around 300 BC, Euclid's \emph{Elements} (Book IX, Proposition 20) proved that the primes outnumber any given finite collection. Multiply all the primes on your list and add 1. The result has a prime factor that is not on your list. The proof remains one of the most admired in mathematics, and it is short enough to check in a minute.
\end{didyouknow}

\begin{didyouknow}{Always a Prime Between}
In 1845 Joseph Bertrand conjectured that for every $n>1$ there is a prime between $n$ and $2n$, and he checked it for $n$ up to about three million. Pafnuty Chebyshev proved it around 1850 using clever bounds on binomial coefficients. In 1932 the 19-year-old Paul Erd\H{o}s published a famously simple proof.
\end{didyouknow}

\begin{didyouknow}{A 41-Million-Digit Prime}
In October 2024 the record for the largest known prime went to the Mersenne prime $2^{136279841}-1$, with 41{,}024{,}320 decimal digits. Luke Durant discovered it in the Great Internet Mersenne Prime Search, using graphics processors rented in the cloud. Mersenne numbers $2^p-1$ dominate the record list because the Lucas-Lehmer test checks them far faster than primality tests for general numbers.
\end{didyouknow}
